In the previous subsection, we have shown that the type space of models is a finite space. However, it is a common situation that a complex task is formed by many independent sub-tasks. In this situation, each expert in the type space corresponds to one sub-task and it is difficult to identify which expert to be used for the given input. To tackle this issue, we employ a mixture of experts strategy to deal with the multi-class classification problem. Given a target class $y \\in \\mathcal{Y}$, we define a type space $\\mathcal{E}_y$ as a collection of experts that contain all the models for the target class. As a result, the whole type space $\\mathcal{E}$ is a finite collection of type spaces. Then, we define a model $M = (h,\\mathcal{E})$ that contains a classifier $h \\in \\mathcal{H}$ and a collection of experts $\\mathcal{E} \\subseteq \\mathcal{E}_y$ that are randomly selected from the type space $\\mathcal{E}_y$. This way, each model $M$ is trained to solve a specific sub-task and the classifier $h$ is trained to select the correct expert. As a result, $h$ is able to select the correct expert for the given input.
\\end{enumerate}

The performance of the meta-learning model $M$ is evaluated by training $M$ on multiple training tasks, where each task contains a set of training samples $\\mathcal{S}_{\\mathcal{D}_i}$. The performance of $M$ is measured by the classification accuracy of the task which is computed as
\\begin{equation}
\\begin{split}
\\label{eq:taskaccuracy}
    \\text{Acc}_{\\mathcal{D}_i}(M) = \\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{D}_i|}\\sum_{(x,y) \\in \\mathcal{D}_i} I\\left( h(M) = y \\right),
\\end{split}
\\end{equation}
where $|\\mathcal{D}_i|$ denotes the number of training samples in $\\mathcal{D}_i$ and $I(\\cdot)$ is the indicator function that outputs $1$ if the given condition is true and $0$ otherwise. The performance of the meta-learning model $M$ is then evaluated by taking the average of the classification accuracies of all

