 is a valid way to pose the question, however, in this article, we will not be focusing on the implementation of such a method, instead we will be focusing on the main difference between CNNs and other machine learning methods. We will be focusing on the concept of parallelization, a method that allows CNNs to perform inference on multiple inputs in parallel, increasing performance.

A key concept to keep in mind when thinking about CNNs is that, the use of convolution and max pooling layers within the architecture are essentially operators that define the pattern of correlations that we wish to find within the input. For example, the filters of a convolutional layer act as kernels that find patterns of correlations across the input, with the max-pooling layer acting as a downsampling operation.

If we look at the original CNN, we can see that it's architecture allows for a small part of the input to be analyzed in parallel, however, the individual computations of each part of the input is still required to complete the analysis.

\\begin{figure}
  \\centering
  \\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{images/CNNarchitecture.png}
  \\caption{Original CNN architecture}
  \\label{fig:CNNarchitecture}
\\end{figure}

We can also consider a more complex CNN architecture that takes the concept of parallelization to the next level, a so-called parallel CNN (or P-CNN), this architecture was designed to analyze the input as a whole at the same time.

\\begin{figure}
  \\centering
  \\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{images/P-CNNarchitecture.png}
  \\caption{Parallel CNN architecture}
  \\label{fig:PCNNarchitecture}
\\end{figure}

Parallel CNNs are able to do so, as we can see in the image above, each layer of the network is defined as a vector that maps to each node in the previous layer. With this representation, we can easily parallelize the analysis of the input.

The process of creating the parallel CNN architecture requires first to decompose the input into blocks that can be analyzed in parallel. The decomposed blocks are then transformed into a set of maps that define how the layer is applied to each block. Finally, these maps are converted into vectors that can be analyzed as a whole.

This type of architecture is not limited to CNNs, any other machine learning model can be used to create the parallelized representation, however, it is the mapping of a given layer to the decomposition of the input that defines the nature of the model

