---

Your first method doesn't work because `User::where()` doesn't work that way. You can use the `first()` method to retrieve the first matching user, but that will not be the current user. To get the current user you should use the `auth` facade and `Auth::user()`. The result will be an instance of the `User` model. This method does not return a json object. If you want to return a json object you should use the `toJson()` method of the `User` model. Here is a working example:

```php
function me()
{
    return \\Auth::user()->toJson();
}
```

